<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karun River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karun River is the largest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab">Shatt al-Arab</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/band-e-kaisar">Band-e Kaisar</a> dam was built across it near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shushtar">Shushtar</a>, reportedly with the labour and engineering skills of Roman prisoners. It illustrates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>'s irrigation works.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karun-river.jpg" alt="Karun River" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shannon1 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karun_river_basin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Karun is the Iranian river with the highest water flow and the country's only navigable river, about 950 kilometres long. It rises in the Zard Kuh mountains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiari">Bakhtiari</a> district in the Zagros Range, on slopes of 4,221 metres, and receives many tributaries such as the Dez and the Kuhrang. It flows past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shushtar">Shushtar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a>, the capital of Khuzestan Province, and reaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab">Arvand Rud</a>, or Shatt al-Arab, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Khorramshahr</a>. Near its mouth the delta forks into two branches, the Bahmanshir and the Haffar, which enclose the island of Abadan.</p><p>In early classical times the river was called the Pasitigris, and Greek writers also knew it as the Eulaeus, the Hebrew Ulai. The name Karun is a corruption of Kuhrang, the name of its source peak and of one of its main tributaries. Medieval Arab and Persian geographers also called it Dujail, which could be translated as Little Tigris.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Karun lies between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> plain, site of cultures going back to the fifth millennium BCE, and the plain of the Zohreh River to the south, where a culture of the same period is now being excavated, notably at Tal-e Choga Sofla. The Elamite civilisation later held the valley. Mesopotamian powers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> at several points, but the Elamite state lasted until about 640 BCE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> overran it and destroyed Susa, one of its largest cities.</p><p>In the 3rd century CE, in the Sassanid era, the first known major bridge across the river was built by Roman captives, among them the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a>. It gave its name to the bridge and dam called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/band-e-kaisar">Band-e Kaisar</a>, Caesar's dam, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shushtar">Shushtar</a>. In one of several competing theories on the location of the Garden of Eden, the Karun is taken to be the Gihon River of Genesis. In 1888 the Lynch Brothers opened the first regular steamship service on the river, linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Khorramshahr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river's watershed covers 65,230 square kilometres, and it provides irrigation to over 280,000 hectares of plain. Much of the transport and resources of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> are tied to it, and since the discovery of oil at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masjed-soleyman">Masjed Soleyman</a> it has been an important route for petroleum to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. Several dams on the main stem, among them Gotvand, Masjed Soleyman, Karun-1, Karun-3 and Karun-4, generate hydroelectric power and provide flood control. The largest city on the river is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shapur's foundation of Bishapur and the Band-e Kaisar dam across the <b>Karun River</b> at Shushtar are both associated with Roman captive labour and expertise, and show how captured knowledge was deliberately put to use.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Banu Ka'b, an Arab tribe from southern Iraq, moved into the marshes of the lower <b>Karun</b> in Khuzestan in the late 17th and early 18th centuries, as the Musha'sha weakened.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-kab-sheikhdom">Banu Ka'b sheikhdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The state rested on Arab tribal warriors and the date and rice lands of the <b>Karun</b> and Karkheh rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/mushasha">Musha'sha</a></li><li><blockquote>The Muhaysin, a branch of the Banu Ka'b sheikhdom, developed the port of Mohammerah (modern Khorramshahr) at the confluence of the <b>Karun</b> and the Shatt al-Arab in the early 19th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-mohammerah">Sheikhdom of Mohammerah</a></li></ul></section>
